345 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 102 0 R/E(Youthful, natural, and ingenuous, the first three committee sessions of the UN Disarmament and International Security Committee \(DISEC,\) were full of vigor and enthusiasm which resulted in an engaging session of debate and deliberation on the given agenda: Establishing a Nuclear Free Zone in South-East Asia.)/Pg 18 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

346 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 102 0 R/E(The first committee session, started slowly but sprang into action with an assertive speech by the delegate of Israel. While the speech became grounds for unsolicited questions, the delegate of Israel craftily revised and defended its national policies. Delegates of countries such as Iceland, Spain, and Australia promised adherence to Article VI of the Non-Proliferation Treaty \(NPT\) and urged others to do the same. The delegate of the United States of America \(USA\) in her GSL speech highlighted the efforts the country had made to curb nuclear proliferation and mentioned the Rapacki Plan, first initiated by Poland and Article VII of the NPT. The chairperson noted that the committee had sternly deviated from the agenda at hand.)/Pg 18 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

347 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 102 0 R/E(In the second committee session, the delegate of Sri Lanka raised a Moderated Caucus on the topic - \215Factors enforcing nuclear weapon development for a country,\216 which saw speeches from 24 delegates. )/Pg 18 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

348 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 102 0 R/E(. The delegates of countries such as Syria, Kenya, Egypt, Russia, and Saudi Arabia among others highlighted how the fear of being suppressed or wrecked by superior or developed countries, leads to the development of nuclear deterrents by such countries, with special emphasis on the Southeast Asian countries. Delegates of countries such as Canada, China, and the Philippines on the other hand emphasized the need for deliberation and alliances rather than taking up nuclear \217deterrents.\220 The delegate of the United Kingdom \(UK\) deliberated upon the need to be concerned about nuclear security, as \217terrorist groups may attempt to acquire nuclear weapon materials through illicit transactions.\220)/Pg 18 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

349 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 102 0 R/E(The committee had shifted into its front gear by the commencement of the third session on the second day, with each delegate having a unique opinion on the agenda at hand. The GSL saw blistering speeches from delegates of countries such as the Republic of Korea, Venezuela, Australia, Malaysia, and Singapore. The delegate of Russia pressed upon the Indo-Pak and Sino-Indian situation, warning how dangerous both were for billions of civilians in South Asia. The delegate of Saudi Arabia came under fire for asking for \217...a treaty to make sure Southeast Asia does not receive nuclear weapons,\220 while the Bangkok treaty was already in action.)/Pg 18 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

350 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 102 0 R/E(Breaking into the fourth session, the delegate of China took on the ante, calling out the 123 Agreement between the USA and India. The delegate also raised a moderated caucus on the topic \205 \215Relationship between ASEAN regarding the South China Sea Dispute,\216 drawing the committee one notch higher and promising an ostentatious conclusion. If the committee would be able to deliberate and maintain the agenda, would be for time to tell.)/Pg 18 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

351 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H6/P 102 0 R/T(CONFLICT OF THE MIND, TENSION ON THE SEA.)/E(CONFLICT OF THE MIND, TENSION ON THE SEA.)/Pg 18 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

